-- title:
--   Mod-I two-cusp forms as a base change from mod p
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, let $M\ge 1$ with $p\mid M$ and $p^{2}\nmid M$, and let $H\le(\mathbb Z/M)^{\times}$ be a subgroup containing every unit whose image in $(\mathbb Z/(M/p))^{\times}$ under `ZMod.unitsMap` is $1$. Let $A\subseteq\mathbb C$ be a subring and $I\subseteq A$ an ideal with $p\in I$, so that $A/I$ carries an $\mathbb F_p$-algebra structure. For a subring $A'\subseteq\mathbb C$ write $L_{A'}$ for the $A'$-span, inside the space of weight-$2$ cusp forms for $\Gamma_H(M)=$ [`CohCarrier.GammaH M H`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133), of those $f$ such that for every element $t$ of the Hecke ring generated by the $T_\ell$ ($\ell$ prime, $\ell\nmid M$), the $U_q$ ($q$ prime, $q\mid M$) and the $\langle d\rangle$, every Atkin–Lehner datum $W$ at $p$ and every $n$, the $n$-th $q$-expansion coefficients of $tf$ and of $W$-slashed $tf$ lie in $A'$. The assertion is that there is an isomorphism of $A/I$-modules $$\beta\colon (A/I)\otimes_{\mathbb F_p}\bigl(L_{\mathbb Z}/pL_{\mathbb Z}\bigr)\;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\;L_A/IL_A$$ (with $L_{\mathbb Z}$ the lattice for the bottom subring and $p$ the ideal `intIdeal p`) such that, first, $\beta(a\otimes \overline f)=a\cdot\overline f$ for every $a\in A/I$ and every $f$ in the integral set for the bottom subring that also lies in $L_A$, the two classes being taken mod $pL_{\mathbb Z}$ and mod $IL_A$ respectively; and second, for every set $S\subseteq\mathbb N$ and every generator $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (a $T_\ell$ with $\ell$ prime, $\ell\notin S$, $\ell\nmid M$; a $U_q$ with $q$ prime dividing $M$; or a $\langle d\rangle$), $\beta$ intertwines the base change to $A/I$ of the operator induced by $g$ on $L_{\mathbb Z}/pL_{\mathbb Z}$ with the operator induced by $g$ on $L_A/IL_A$.
--
--   This identifies the mod-$I$ two-cusp cusp-form module over an arbitrary subring $A\subseteq\mathbb C$ with the base change along $\mathbb F_p\to A/I$ of the mod-$p$ integral module, compatibly with the Hecke and diamond operators; it rests on the existence of a $\mathbb C$-basis of weight-$2$ cusp forms that spans the two-cusp lattice over every subring of $\mathbb C$ simultaneously. It is used in the comparison of these modules with the dual of the multiplicative part of the Tate module of $J_H$ in the ordinary case.
